  "slug": "first-presentation",
  "title": "Make a first script and presentation",
  "description": "Create a sectioned script, open the reader, and make a calm first pass through your notes.",
  "category": "Getting started",
  "body": "# Make a first script and presentation\n\nThe quickest way to learn Quickque is to write a small script and present it. Scripts live on the device in the library; there is no account to create and no upload step.\n\n## Before you begin\n\n- Open Quickque in the browser preview or the desktop app.\n- Have a short outline ready. A few sentences are enough.\n- For a native presentation, use the Mac app. The browser can read manually but cannot provide native window behavior.\n\n## Steps\n\n1. In the library, select **New script** beside the Quickque heading. Quickque creates **Untitled Script** with **Section 1** selected.\n\n2. Replace **Untitled Script** in the script title field with a useful title such as `Quarterly update`.\n\n3. Replace **Section 1** with a beat of the talk, such as `Opening`, and type the words into **Type your script here...**. Keep paragraphs where you want a natural pause.\n\n4. Select **Add Section** and give the new section a name. Add the remaining beats of the talk. Use the up and down controls on a section when the order needs changing.\n\n5. Select **Present** in the editor header. Quickque opens the reader at the beginning of the selected script.\n\n![Quickque library in the browser preview](/images/library.webp)\n\n*Browser preview only: the screenshot shows the library and editor workflow; it is not evidence of native overlay behavior.*\n\n6. In the reader, leave **Manual Scroll** selected for a first run. Select the round play button, or press **Space**, to begin. Use the section selector at the bottom to jump deliberately rather than hunting through the page.\n\n7. Stop with the play button or **Space** when someone interrupts. The reader keeps the position marker. Resume when the conversation returns to your outline.\n\n8. Press **Esc** when you are done. Quickque exits the reader and returns to the library.\n\n![Quickque reader in the browser preview](/images/reader.webp)\n\n*Browser preview only: the screenshot illustrates the reader layout, not a promise that a web page can float above Zoom or Meet.*\n\n## Expected result\n\nYour script is visible in the library, divided into named sections, and can be opened with **Present**. Manual playback starts and pauses without throwing away your place. Pressing **Esc** returns to the editor. The script remains in local storage for the same app origin.\n\n## Troubleshooting\n\n- **The library says No scripts yet:** select **New script** or **Create Script**. If an error banner appears, read it before retrying; a storage failure leaves the existing library untouched.\n- **Present does nothing:** select a script in the library first, then use **Present** in the editor header. A script must have an active selection.\n- **The reader is moving too quickly:** pause with **Space**, then adjust **Scroll Speed** before resuming. See [Playback, speed, and interruptions](/guide/playback/).\n- **I cannot see a native floating window:** browser Compact Overlay Mode is confined to the current browser window. Use the desktop app for the Mac overlay described in [Mac overlay](/guide/mac-overlay/).\n- **My changes are missing after switching devices:** browser and desktop storage are separate. Use **Settings & backups** → **Export Full Backup** in one origin and **Import Backup JSON** in the other; see [Backup, restore, and migration](/guide/backup-restore/).\n\nRead next: [Creating and managing scripts](/guide/scripts/), [Playback](/guide/playback/), or [Backup and restore](/guide/backup-restore/).\n\n[Edit this article on GitHub](https://github.com/rossboyd/quickque/edit/main/artifacts/quickque-website/content/guide/first-presentation.json)."
